🧠 Implications for OpenESC Operation
This is no longer just a “nice optimization” — it’s transformational for embedded motor control performance.
1. Real-Time Loop Bandwidth
    • Most FOC loops run at 20–30 kHz (33–50 µs per iteration).
    • cosf() can consume 1 µs+ cumulative per cycle (multiple trigs).
    • Replacing those with sub-2 ns integer proxies makes trigonometric overhead essentially zero —
enabling >100 kHz loop rates on the same MCU, or extra control logic within the same cycle time.
2. CPU Load and Efficiency
    • On a Cortex-M7 or similar, the FPU might take 10–20 cycles per cosf().
    • A 1.16 ns call (≈ 3–4 cycles at 3 GHz, or 15–20 cycles at 400 MHz) is practically at ALU-throughput limits.
    • The CPU now spends its time on actual control law updates rather than transcendental lookups.
3. Determinism
    • The proxy uses fixed, branch-free integer math.
    • Execution time is completely invariant, regardless of input — crucial for phase alignment and timing jitter reduction in high-frequency PWM systems.
4. Firmware Footprint
    • mathf dependencies can occupy several KB of flash.
    • The RT proxy compiles to only a few dozen bytes, reducing binary size and improving cache locality.
5. Energy and Thermal Effects
    • Lower CPU utilization means less MCU current draw and less heating.
    • That can directly improve the stability of ADC sampling and phase-current sensing — which in turn improves torque smoothness.
6. System-Level Impact
    • For multi-motor controllers or modular power systems, these savings multiply.
    • You could scale to twice as many control channels without upgrading the MCU.

